 "standard_text": "Recall and demonstrate proficiency with multiplication facts for 3, 4, 5 and 10; extend and apply facts to develop the related division facts",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 3 maths worksheets on the multiplication facts for 3, 4, 5 and 10: equal groups, arrays, skip counting, turnarounds, related division facts and word problems. 100 questions with an answer key, following the Australian Curriculum and useful for NAPLAN.",
 "key_big": "Double the 2s to get the 4s. Halve the 10s to get the 5s.",
 "key_sub": "For the 3s, double and add one more group: 3 × 7 = 14 + 7 = 21. Turnarounds have the same answer (4 × 7 = 7 × 4), and every fact gives a division fact: 28 ÷ 4 = 7.",
 "body_html": "<h2>Multiplication is equal groups</h2><p><b>4 × 3</b> means 4 groups of 3. Skip count by 3s: 3, 6, 9, 12. So 4 × 3 = 12. Turn it around and the answer stays the same: 3 × 4 = 12 too.</p><figure class='sn-fig'><svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 362 60' width='362' height='60' role='img' aria-label='4 equal groups with 3 counters in each group'><g font-family='Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif'><rect x='2' y='2' width='82' height='34' rx='8' fill='#e8f3ec' stroke='#006633' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='19' cy='19' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='43' cy='19' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='67' cy='19' r='8' fill='#006633'/><text x='43.0' y='53.0' font-size='15' font-weight='700' fill='#2b2b2b' text-anchor='middle'>3</text><rect x='94' y='2' width='82' height='34' rx='8' fill='#e8f3ec' stroke='#006633' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='111' cy='19' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='135' cy='19' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='159' cy='19' r='8' fill='#006633'/><text x='135.0' y='53.0' font-size='15' font-weight='700' fill='#2b2b2b' text-anchor='middle'>3</text><rect x='186' y='2' width='82' height='34' rx='8' fill='#e8f3ec' stroke='#006633' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='203' cy='19' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='227' cy='19' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='251' cy='19' r='8' fill='#006633'/><text x='227.0' y='53.0' font-size='15' font-weight='700' fill='#2b2b2b' text-anchor='middle'>3</text><rect x='278' y='2' width='82' height='34' rx='8' fill='#e8f3ec' stroke='#006633' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='295' cy='19' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='319' cy='19' r='8' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='343' cy='19' r='8' fill='#006633'/><text x='319.0' y='53.0' font-size='15' font-weight='700' fill='#2b2b2b' text-anchor='middle'>3</text></g></svg><figcaption>4 groups of 3: 3, 6, 9, 12. 4 × 3 = 12.</figcaption></figure><h2>A trick for each fact family</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Facts</th><th>Trick</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>× 3</td><td>Double, then add one more group</td><td>3 × 7: 14 + 7 = 21</td></tr><tr><td>× 4</td><td>Double, then double again</td><td>4 × 6: 12, 24</td></tr><tr><td>× 10</td><td>Count the tens; the answer ends in 0</td><td>10 × 6 = 60</td></tr><tr><td>× 5</td><td>Half of the 10s; the answer ends in 0 or 5</td><td>5 × 6: half of 60 = 30</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class='sn-fig'><svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 244 44' width='244' height='44' role='img' aria-label='6 coins worth 5 cents each'><g font-family='Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif'><circle cx='22.0' cy='22.0' r='16' fill='#d9dde2' stroke='#7a828c' stroke-width='2'/><text x='22.0' y='27.5' font-size='15' font-weight='700' fill='#2b2b2b' text-anchor='middle'>5c</text><circle cx='62.0' cy='22.0' r='16' fill='#d9dde2' stroke='#7a828c' stroke-width='2'/><text x='62.0' y='27.5' font-size='15' font-weight='700' fill='#2b2b2b' text-anchor='middle'>5c</text><circle cx='102.0' cy='22.0' r='16' fill='#d9dde2' stroke='#7a828c' stroke-width='2'/><text x='102.0' y='27.5' font-size='15' font-weight='700' fill='#2b2b2b' text-anchor='middle'>5c</text><circle cx='142.0' cy='22.0' r='16' fill='#d9dde2' stroke='#7a828c' stroke-width='2'/><text x='142.0' y='27.5' font-size='15' font-weight='700' fill='#2b2b2b' text-anchor='middle'>5c</text><circle cx='182.0' cy='22.0' r='16' fill='#d9dde2' stroke='#7a828c' stroke-width='2'/><text x='182.0' y='27.5' font-size='15' font-weight='700' fill='#2b2b2b' text-anchor='middle'>5c</text><circle cx='222.0' cy='22.0' r='16' fill='#d9dde2' stroke='#7a828c' stroke-width='2'/><text x='222.0' y='27.5' font-size='15' font-weight='700' fill='#2b2b2b' text-anchor='middle'>5c</text></g></svg><figcaption>Six 5c coins: 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30. 6 × 5c = 30c.</figcaption></figure><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th></th><th>3s</th><th>4s</th><th>5s</th><th>10s</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>1 × 3 = 3</td><td>1 × 4 = 4</td><td>1 × 5 = 5</td><td>1 × 10 = 10</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2 × 3 = 6</td><td>2 × 4 = 8</td><td>2 × 5 = 10</td><td>2 × 10 = 20</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>3 × 3 = 9</td><td>3 × 4 = 12</td><td>3 × 5 = 15</td><td>3 × 10 = 30</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>4 × 3 = 12</td><td>4 × 4 = 16</td><td>4 × 5 = 20</td><td>4 × 10 = 40</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>5 × 3 = 15</td><td>5 × 4 = 20</td><td>5 × 5 = 25</td><td>5 × 10 = 50</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>6 × 3 = 18</td><td>6 × 4 = 24</td><td>6 × 5 = 30</td><td>6 × 10 = 60</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>7 × 3 = 21</td><td>7 × 4 = 28</td><td>7 × 5 = 35</td><td>7 × 10 = 70</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>8 × 3 = 24</td><td>8 × 4 = 32</td><td>8 × 5 = 40</td><td>8 × 10 = 80</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>9 × 3 = 27</td><td>9 × 4 = 36</td><td>9 × 5 = 45</td><td>9 × 10 = 90</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>10 × 3 = 30</td><td>10 × 4 = 40</td><td>10 × 5 = 50</td><td>10 × 10 = 100</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>4 × 8 = ?</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Double 8 is 16. Double 16 is 32.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 32</p></div><h2>Every fact gives division facts</h2><p>Division is the opposite of multiplication. 3, 7 and 21 make a <b>fact family</b>: 3 × 7 = 21, 7 × 3 = 21, 21 ÷ 3 = 7 and 21 ÷ 7 = 3.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Division</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>28 ÷ 4 = ?</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Think: 4 × ? = 28. 4 × 7 = 28.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 7</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Missing number</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>? × 5 = 45</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Think: 45 ÷ 5. Skip count by 5s to 45: that is 9 jumps.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 9</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Word problem</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>A tricycle has 3 wheels. How many wheels do 5 tricycles have?</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>5 groups of 3: 3, 6, 9, 12, 15. So 5 × 3 = 15.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 15 wheels</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Using the fact next door: 4 × 7 = 24</td><td>Double 7 is 14, double 14 is 28</td></tr><tr><td>Adding instead: 5 × 3 = 8</td><td>× means groups of: 5 groups of 3 = 15</td></tr><tr><td>Skip counting one step too far: 5 × 6 = 35</td><td>Count the jumps on your fingers: 6 jumps of 5 is 30</td></tr><tr><td>Giving the big number in division: 28 ÷ 4 = 28</td><td>Ask: 4 times what makes 28? The answer is 7</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What multiplication facts should a Year 3 student know in Australia?",
   "a": "The Australian Curriculum (v9.0) asks Year 3 students to recall the multiplication facts for 3, 4, 5 and 10 and use them to work out the related division facts. In Year 4 this grows to all facts up to 10 × 10."
  },
  {
   "q": "Is multiplication on the Year 3 NAPLAN numeracy test?",
   "a": "Yes. The Year 3 numeracy test can include simple multiplication and division, often as short word problems. Quick recall of these facts gives students more time to think about the problem."
  },
  {
   "q": "How can I help my child learn the 3 and 4 times tables?",
   "a": "Skip count out loud, then practise a few facts each day. For the 4s, double and double again: 4 × 6 is 12, then 24. For the 3s, double the number and add one more: 3 × 7 is 14 + 7 = 21."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why learn the 5s and 10s together?",
   "a": "The 5s are half of the 10s. If your child knows 10 × 8 = 80, then 5 × 8 is half of 80, which is 40. Counting 5c and 10c coins is a good way to practise both."
  }
 ],
